<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib58.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Advances in Multimedia Information Processing-PCM 2008: 9th
Pacific Rim Conference on Multimedia, Tainan, Taiwan, December 9-13, 2008.
Proceedings 9</em>, pages 721–730. Springer.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib59" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Xu et al. (2023)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Zhaozhen Xu, Zhijin Guo, and Nello Cristianini. 2023.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">On compositionality in data embedding.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib59.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Advances in Intelligent Data Analysis XXI: 21st
International Symposium, IDA 2023</em>. Springer.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib60" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Yang et al. (2014)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Bishan Yang, Wen-tau Yih, Xiaodong He, Jianfeng Gao, and Li Deng. 2014.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Embedding entities and relations for learning and inference in
knowledge bases.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib60.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arXiv preprint arXiv:1412.6575</em>.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib61" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Yazdani et al. (2015)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Majid Yazdani, Meghdad Farahmand, and James Henderson. 2015.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Learning semantic composition to detect non-compositionality of
multiword expressions.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib61.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proceedings of the 2015 conference on empirical methods in
natural language processing</em>, pages 1733–1742.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Details of Correlation-Based Compositionality Detection Method</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) is used to measure the correlation information between two multivariate random variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shawe-Taylor et al., 2004</a>)</cite>. Just like the univariate correlation coefficient, it is estimated on the basis of two aligned samples of observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A matrix of binary-valued attribute embeddings, denoted as <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, is essentially a matrix representation where each row corresponds to a specific attribute and each column corresponds to an individual data point (such as a word, image, or user). The entries of the matrix can take only two values, typically 0 or 1, signifying the absence or presence of a particular attribute. For example, in the context of textual data, an attribute might represent whether a word is a noun or not, and the matrix would be populated with 1s (presence) and 0s (absence) accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, a matrix of user embeddings, denoted as <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a matrix where each row represents an individual user, and each column represents a certain feature or dimension of the embedding space. These embeddings are continuous-valued vectors that capture the movie preference of the users. The values in this matrix are not constrained to binary values and can span a continuous range.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These paired random variables are often different descriptions of the same object, for example genetic and clinical information about a set of patients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seoane et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, French and English translations of the same document <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vinokourov et al., 2002</a>)</cite>, and even two images of the same object from different angles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo and Wu, 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the example of viewers and movies, we use this method to compare two descriptions of users. One matrix is based on demographic information, which are indicated by Boolean vectors. The other matrix is based on their behaviour, which is computed by their movie ratings only.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming we have a vector for an individual user’s attribute embedding, denoted as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}=\left(a_{1},a_{2},\dots,a_{n}\right)^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}=\left(a_{1},a_{2},\dots,a_{n}\right)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and a corresponding individual user’s computed embedding:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=\left(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{m}\right)^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=\left(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{m}\right)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">we aim to explore the correlation between these two representations across multiple users. Given a set of <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> users, we define <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix where each row corresponds to the attribute embeddings for a specific user, and <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix where each row represents the computed embedding of the same user. Here, <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of attributes, and <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of the user embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) is then employed to find the projection matrices <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximise the correlation between the transformed representations of <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each projection matrix contains <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-projection vectors, where <math id="A1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of canonical components that depend on the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix. For each <math id="A1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical component, the projections of the attribute and user embeddings are given by:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{U}_{k}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐀𝐰</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐔𝐰</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{U}_{k}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="A1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th projection vectors from the matrices <math id="A1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. <math id="A1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the projection of the original attribute matrix <math id="A1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> (size <math id="A1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times n</annotation></semantics></math>) onto the <math id="A1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical direction, using the projection vector <math id="A1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It results in a vector of size <math id="A1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> that contains the transformed values for each user for the <math id="A1.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th component. Similarly, <math id="A1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the projection of the original user embedding matrix <math id="A1.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> (size <math id="A1.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times m</annotation></semantics></math>) onto the <math id="A1.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical direction, using the projection vector <math id="A1.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. It also results in a vector of size <math id="A1.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> that contains the transformed values for each user for the <math id="A1.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th component.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of CCA is to maximise the Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC) between these transformed representations, i.e., between <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical component. The correlation for the <math id="A1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical component, denoted as <math id="A1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by the formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{A}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}\right)\left(\left(\mathbf{U}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}\right)}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{A}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}\right)^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{U}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}\right)^{2}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{A}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}\right)\left(\left(\mathbf{U}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}\right)}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{A}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}\right)^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{q}\left(\left(\mathbf{U}_{k}\right)_{i}-\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}\right)^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathbf{A}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathbf{U}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the means of the transformed attribute and user embeddings, respectively, and <math id="A1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of users.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In matrix form, we can express this objective as maximizing the correlation between the transformed matrices <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\mathbf{W}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\mathbf{W}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\mathbf{W}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\mathbf{W}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is formalized as:</p>
<table id="A1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\max_{\mathbf{W}_{A},\mathbf{W}_{U}}\operatorname{corr}\left(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{W}_{A},\mathbf{U}\mathbf{W}_{U}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>corr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\max_{\mathbf{W}_{A},\mathbf{W}_{U}}\operatorname{corr}\left(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{W}_{A},\mathbf{U}\mathbf{W}_{U}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where the correlation is maximised across the projection matrices <math id="A1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the result is a set of <math id="A1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-canonical correlations <math id="A1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1},\rho_{2},\dots,\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1},\rho_{2},\dots,\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> that describe the relationship between the attribute embeddings and the user embeddings for the entire dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, by computing the canonical correlations <math id="A1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> for each component, we obtain insights into how well the attribute embeddings and computed user embeddings are aligned in terms of their underlying structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the case where we have 4 users. The attribute matrix <math id="A1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimensions <math id="A1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math>, representing 4 users and 4 attributes, and the user embedding matrix <math id="A1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimensions <math id="A1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>, representing 4 users and 3 embedding dimensions. Thus:
- <math id="A1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 4}</annotation></semantics></math>: the attribute matrix where each row represents the 4-dimensional attribute vector for a user.
- <math id="A1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>: the user embedding matrix where each row represents the 3-dimensional embedding vector for a user.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) is employed to find the projection matrices <math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximize the correlation between the transformed representations of <math id="A1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each projection matrix contains <math id="A1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-projection vectors, where <math id="A1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of canonical components that depend on the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix. For each <math id="A1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th canonical component, the projections of the attribute and user embeddings are given by:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{U}_{k}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐀𝐰</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐔𝐰</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{U}_{k}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p14.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{a_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{u_{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="A1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th projection vectors from the matrices <math id="A1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Leave-one-out experiment</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Leave Out</span>: Exclude the <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row from <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{-i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{-i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train</span>: Solve <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{-i}\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{U}_{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{-i}\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{U}_{-i}</annotation></semantics></math> using the pseudo-inverse to obtain <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Predict</span>: Estimate the left-out embedding using <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}=\mathbf{a}_{i}\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}=\mathbf{a}_{i}\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the attribute vector of entity <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluate</span>: Compare <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the actual embedding <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using the following metrics:</p>
<ol id="A2.I1.i4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">L2 Loss</em>: <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}=\|\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}-\mathbf{u}_{i}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}=\|\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}-\mathbf{u}_{i}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, measuring the reconstruction error.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Embedding Prediction (Cosine Similarity)</em>: <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos(\theta)=\dfrac{\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{u}_{i}}{\|\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}\|\,\|\mathbf{u}_{i}\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos(\theta)=\dfrac{\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{u}_{i}}{\|\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}\|\,\|\mathbf{u}_{i}\|}</annotation></semantics></math>, assessing the alignment between the predicted and actual embeddings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Identity Prediction (Retrieval Accuracy)</em>: Determines if <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> correctly identifies entity <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by checking if <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the nearest neighbor to <math id="A2.I1.i4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> among all embeddings.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Knowledge Graph Embedding</h2>

<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge Graph Embedding</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">graph</span> <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a set of vertices <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with edges <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> between pairs of vertices. In a <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">knowledge graph</span>, the vertices <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> represent entities in the real world, and the edges <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> encode that some relation holds between a pair of vertices. As a running example, we consider the case where the vertices <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are a set of viewers and films, and the edges <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> encode the fact that a viewer has rated a film.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge Graphs represent information in terms of entities (or nodes) and the relationships (or edges) between them. The specific relation <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> that exists between two entities is depicted as a directed edge, and this connection is represented by a triple <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this structure, we distinguish between the two nodes involved: the <em id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">head</em> (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>) and the <em id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tail</em> (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), represented by vectors <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Such a triple is termed a <em id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fact</em>, denoted by <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=(h,r,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Embedding Function.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A knowledge graph <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> can be embedded by assigning each node <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> a vector <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This embedding function, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{KG}:V\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{KG}:V\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, maps nodes into a continuous space where their relationships are captured by a scoring or distance function.
For instance, one could define a threshold <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that an edge <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i},v_{j})\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i},v_{j})\in E</annotation></semantics></math> exists if and only if <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mathbf{x}_{v_{i}},\mathbf{x}_{v_{j}})&lt;\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mathbf{x}_{v_{i}},\mathbf{x}_{v_{j}})&lt;\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, given a set of embedded points, links between nodes can be recovered by applying a learned scoring function <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\bigl(\mathbf{x}_{v_{i}},\mathbf{x}_{v_{j}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\bigl(\mathbf{x}_{v_{i}},\mathbf{x}_{v_{j}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Multiplicative Scoring</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nickel et al. (2011)</a></cite> introduced a tensor-factorization approach for relational learning, treating each frontal slice of a three-dimensional tensor as a co-occurrence matrix for a given relation.
In this model, a triple <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is scored using embeddings <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h},\mathbf{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h},\mathbf{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represent head and tail entities, and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the relation:</p>
<table id="A3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(h,r,t)\;=\;\mathbf{h}^{T}\,\mathbf{R}\,\mathbf{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(h,r,t)\;=\;\mathbf{h}^{T}\,\mathbf{R}\,\mathbf{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Various specializations exist, such as DistMult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, which restricts <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to a diagonal matrix (reducing overfitting), and ComplEx <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trouillon et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, which employs complex-valued embeddings for asymmetric relations.
In this work, we adopt DistMult due to its simplicity and scalability, particularly its suitability for large knowledge graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Additive Scoring</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TransE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordes et al., 2013</a>)</cite> introduces a translation-based perspective, where each relation is a vector that shifts the embedding of the head entity to the tail entity.
A triple <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is scored by:</p>
<table id="A3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(h,r,t)\;=\;\|\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{r}\;-\;\mathbf{t}\|,\quad\mathbf{h},\mathbf{r},\mathbf{t}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(h,r,t)\;=\;\|\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{r}\;-\;\mathbf{t}\|,\quad\mathbf{h},\mathbf{r},\mathbf{t}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For example, <em id="A3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">King</em> + <em id="A3.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">FemaleOf</em> <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A3.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Queen</em>. This translation idea captures relational semantics by minimizing the distance between <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rating Prediction</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In alignment with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berg et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, we establish a function <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that, given a triple of embeddings <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, calculates the probability of the relation against all potential alternatives.</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P\left(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}\right)=\text{SoftArgmax}(S(f))=\frac{e^{S(f)}}{e^{S(f)}+\sum_{r^{\prime}\neq r\in\mathscr{R}}e^{S(f^{\prime})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SoftArgmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P\left(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}\right)=\text{SoftArgmax}(S(f))=\frac{e^{S(f)}}{e^{S(f)}+\sum_{r^{\prime}\neq r\in\mathscr{R}}e^{S(f^{\prime})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the above formula, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a true triple, and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}=(h,r^{\prime},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=(h,r^{\prime},t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a corrupted triple, that is a randomly generated one, that we use as a proxy for a negative example (a pair of nodes that are not connected).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assigning numerical values to relations <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the predicted relation is then just the expected value <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{prediction}=\sum_{r\in\mathscr{R}}rP\left(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>prediction</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{prediction}=\sum_{r\in\mathscr{R}}rP\left(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
In our application of viewers and movies, the set of relations <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}</annotation></semantics></math> could be the possible ratings that a user can give a movie. The predicted rating is then the expected value of the ratings, given the probability distribution produced by the scoring function. <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f)</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the scoring function in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2014)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To learn a graph embedding, we follow the setting of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bose and Hamilton (2019)</a></cite> as follows,</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L=-\sum_{f\in\mathscr{F}}\log\frac{e^{S(f)}}{e^{S(f)}+\sum_{f^{\prime}\in\mathscr{F}^{\prime}}e^{S(f^{\prime})}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=-\sum_{f\in\mathscr{F}}\log\frac{e^{S(f)}}{e^{S(f)}+\sum_{f^{\prime}\in\mathscr{F}^{\prime}}e^{S(f^{\prime})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This loss function maximizes the probabilities of true triples <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and minimizes the probability of triples with corrupted triples: <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use 4 metrics to evaluate our performance on the link prediction task.
These are root mean square error (RMSE, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}\right)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}\right)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is our predicted relation and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true relation), Hits@K - the probability that our target value is in the top <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> predictions, mean rank (MR) - the average ranking of each prediction, and mean reciprocal rank (MRR) to evaluate our performance on the link prediction task. These are standard metrics in the knowledge graph embedding community.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
