<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Word Embeddings</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>WordNet Embedding</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">WordNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miller, 1995</a>)</cite> is a large lexical database of English that combines dictionary and thesaurus features with a graph structure. It organizes nouns, verbs, adjectives, and adverbs into synsets (sets of cognitive synonyms) interlinked by semantic and lexical relations. We use WN18RR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, a subset of WordNet with 40,943 entities and 11 types of relation. For linearity experiments, we select 25,781 words from the intersection of the WN18RR and word2vec (GoogleNews-vectors-negative300) vocabularies. We train embeddings for these words over WordNet on an entity prediction task, which involves predicting the tail entity given a head entity and relation. Training details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 WordNet Embedding ‣ Appendix F Word Embeddings ‣ Quantifying Compositionality of Classic and State-of-the-Art Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>. We build a continuous embedding matrix <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{w2v-wn}\in\mathbb{R}^{25,781\times 300}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>25,781</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{w2v-wn}\in\mathbb{R}^{25,781\times 300}</annotation></semantics></math> using vectors from GoogleNews-vectors-negative300, and a second continuous embedding matrix <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{wn-w2v}\in\mathbb{R}^{25,781\times 20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>25,781</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{wn-w2v}\in\mathbb{R}^{25,781\times 20}</annotation></semantics></math>.

We want to ensure our WordNet embedding can contain the semantic relation in it. Therefore, we train the embedding with the task of predicting the tail entity given a head entity and relation. For example, we might want to predict the hypernym of cat:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\textbf{cat},hypernym,\textbf{?}&gt;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">cat</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">?</mtext></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\textbf{cat},hypernym,\textbf{?}&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mapping Freebase ID to text</h5>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">WordNet is constructed with Freebase ID only, an example triple could be &lt;00260881, hypernym, 00260622&gt;. We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">villmow (2019)</a></cite> to preprocess the data and map each entity with the text with a real meaning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The above triple can then be processed with the real semantic meaning: &lt;land reform, hypernym, reform&gt;. The word2vec word embedding is pretrained from a google news corpus.
We train the WordNet Embedding in the following way:</p>
<ol id="A6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We split our dataset to use 90% for training, 10% for testing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Triples of <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(head,relation,tail\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(head,relation,tail\right)</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded as relational triples <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(h,r,t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(h,r,t\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We randomly initialize embeddings for each <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, use the scoring function in Equation <a href="#A3.E4" title="In C.2 Additive Scoring ‣ Appendix C Knowledge Graph Embedding ‣ Quantifying Compositionality of Classic and State-of-the-Art Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and minimize the loss by Margin Loss.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sampled 20 corrupted entities. Learning rate is set at 0.05 and training epoch at 300.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Results can be found in the Table <a href="#A6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Mapping Freebase ID to text ‣ F.1 WordNet Embedding ‣ Appendix F Word Embeddings ‣ Quantifying Compositionality of Classic and State-of-the-Art Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, which shows that our WordNet embeddings do contain semantic information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Link prediction performance for WordNet</figcaption>
